cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "neuralmesh: a controlled measurement of under-reaching in mesh graph networks"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Biswas
    given-names: Samarjith
    affiliation: "NewFrontiers of Sound Center, University of Arizona"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Samarjithbiswas/neuralmesh"
url: "https://github.com/Samarjithbiswas/neuralmesh"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-07-30"
keywords:
  - graph neural network
  - message passing
  - under-reaching
  - finite element method
  - surrogate model
  - scientific machine learning
abstract: >-
  A message-passing network with L layers has a receptive field of exactly L hops. For
  an elliptic PDE the solution at an interior node depends on every boundary value, so
  once the graph diameter exceeds L the architecture cannot represent the solution.
  This repository measures whether interleaving global attention removes that
  limitation, under controls that make the answer interpretable: parameter counts
  matched to within 0.7 percent, a no-communication control as a floor, and error
  resolved by distance from the driven boundary. Ground truth is a P1 finite element
  solver included in the repository and verified to second-order convergence. Across
  graph diameters of 20, 40 and 80, attention reduces aggregate error by 42, 40 and 42
  percent against the parameter-matched baseline. The reach-limited baseline does not
  degrade with diameter in the aggregate metric; the effect appears only in the
  distance-resolved metric, where mid-domain error grows 48 and 85 percent for the two
  local models while remaining flat for the global one.
